WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.629
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.629 --> 00:00:08.300
Fluid and smoke simulations are widely used
in computer games and in the movie industry,

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:12.880
and are capable of creating absolutely stunning
video footage.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:17.590
We can very quickly put together a coarse
simulation and run it cheaply, however, the

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:22.640
more turbulent motion we're trying to simulate,
the more resources and time it will take.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:26.670
If we wish to create some footage with the
amount of visual quality that you see here,

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:31.540
well, if you think the several hour computation
time for light transport algorithms was too

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:37.490
much, better hold on to your papers because
it will take not hours, but often from days

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:39.090
to weeks to compute.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:44.530
And to ease the computation time of such simulations,
this is a technique that performs style transfer,

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:49.690
but this time, not for paintings, but for
fluid and smoke simulations!

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:51.440
How cool is that!

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:57.260
It takes the low-resolution source and detailed
target footage, dices them up into small patches

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:02.680
and borrows from image and texture synthesis
techniques to create a higher resolution version

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:04.210
of our input simulation.

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:08.470
The challenge of this technique is that we
cannot just put more swirly motion on top

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:13.319
of our velocity fields because this piece
of fluid has to obey to the laws of physics

00:01:13.319 --> 00:01:14.409
to look natural.

00:01:14.409 --> 00:01:18.980
Also, we have to make sure that there is not
too much variation from patch to patch, so

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:23.109
we have to perform some sort of smoothing
on the boundaries of these patches.

00:01:23.109 --> 00:01:27.890
Also, our smoke plumes also have to interact
with obstacles, which is anything but trivial

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:29.119
to do well.

00:01:29.119 --> 00:01:33.120
Have a look at the ground truth results from
the high resolution simulation - this is the

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.289
one that would take a long time to compute.

00:01:35.289 --> 00:01:40.249
There are clearly deviations, but given how
coarse the input footage was, I'll take this

00:01:40.249 --> 00:01:41.679
any day of the week.

00:01:41.679 --> 00:01:46.600
We can now look forward to seeing even higher
quality smoke and fluids in the animation

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:48.490
movies of the near future.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:52.810
There was a similar technique by the name
Wavelet Turbulence, which is one of my all-time

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:57.789
favorite papers that has been showcased in
the very first Two Minute Papers episode.

00:01:57.789 --> 00:02:01.899
This is what it looked like and we are now
celebrating its tenth anniversary.

00:02:01.899 --> 00:02:06.799
Imagine what a bomb this was ten years ago,
and you know what, it is still going strong.

00:02:06.799 --> 00:02:10.200
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

